WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.910 --> 00:00:08.300
Dr. Keith Devlin is a very big guy in the world of math education,

2
00:00:08.300 --> 00:00:10.880
not to mention the world of math research.

3
00:00:10.880 --> 00:00:13.720
I'm introducing you to him here because I love

4
00:00:13.720 --> 00:00:16.510
how he thinks about learning in the world of mathematics,

5
00:00:16.510 --> 00:00:19.690
and how his ideas about learning relate to learning in

6
00:00:19.690 --> 00:00:23.140
all sorts of topics way outside of mathematics.

7
00:00:23.140 --> 00:00:24.990
Among many other duties,

8
00:00:24.990 --> 00:00:27.640
Keith is a Co-founder and executive director of

9
00:00:27.640 --> 00:00:32.555
Stanford's human sciences and technologies advanced research institute.

10
00:00:32.555 --> 00:00:37.040
A co-founder and president of an educational technology company,

11
00:00:37.040 --> 00:00:41.120
Brain Quake, that creates mathematical learning video games.

12
00:00:41.120 --> 00:00:44.550
He's also a World Economic Forum fellow,

13
00:00:44.550 --> 00:00:48.890
a fellow of the American Association for the Advancement of Science,

14
00:00:48.890 --> 00:00:52.325
and a fellow of the American Mathematical Society.

15
00:00:52.325 --> 00:00:54.825
Last but definitely not least,

16
00:00:54.825 --> 00:00:58.640
he is ''The Math Guy'' on national public radio.

17
00:00:58.640 --> 00:01:00.770
Welcome Doctor Devlin.

18
00:01:00.770 --> 00:01:03.855
Thanks for being with us here today Dr. Devlin,

19
00:01:03.855 --> 00:01:05.540
let's just dive right in.

20
00:01:05.540 --> 00:01:09.620
What's the biggest tip you can share with our viewers about how to

21
00:01:09.620 --> 00:01:14.305
most effectively approach a math problem that they might be stuck on?

22
00:01:14.305 --> 00:01:16.580
Well, the first thing of course is don't panic.

23
00:01:16.580 --> 00:01:21.120
Not-so-famous Douglas Adams motto from The Hitchhiker's Guide to the Galaxy,

24
00:01:21.120 --> 00:01:24.930
and it holds just as much for mathematics as it did for for the Hitchhiker's Guide.

25
00:01:24.930 --> 00:01:27.840
You have to begin by understanding what the problem is about,

26
00:01:27.840 --> 00:01:29.225
what are you trying to do?

27
00:01:29.225 --> 00:01:30.480
What do you want to achieve?

28
00:01:30.480 --> 00:01:32.170
And what information do you have?

29
00:01:32.170 --> 00:01:35.770
You've really got to avoid the thing that actually teachers try to

30
00:01:35.770 --> 00:01:39.520
impress on you of trying to see if you can match it to some template,

31
00:01:39.520 --> 00:01:41.020
and to act quickly.

32
00:01:41.020 --> 00:01:44.470
One of the worst things that I think happens in the school system is

33
00:01:44.470 --> 00:01:46.030
people come away thinking that solving

34
00:01:46.030 --> 00:01:47.970
math problems is something that has to be done at speed.

35
00:01:47.970 --> 00:01:49.220
It absolutely is not.

36
00:01:49.220 --> 00:01:50.490
So, you have to sit back,

37
00:01:50.490 --> 00:01:53.545
take a deep breath, begin by understanding the problem.

38
00:01:53.545 --> 00:01:55.530
If the problem involves numbers,

39
00:01:55.530 --> 00:01:58.030
put in a few simple numbers threes and fours,

40
00:01:58.030 --> 00:01:59.840
and just play with it to get a sense of it.

41
00:01:59.840 --> 00:02:01.440
If it is geometric problem,

42
00:02:01.440 --> 00:02:02.819
do some little diagrams,

43
00:02:02.819 --> 00:02:07.710
I often end up writing simple little graph diagrams with dots and lines connecting them,

44
00:02:07.710 --> 00:02:10.640
just something to give me a sense of what that problem's about.

45
00:02:10.640 --> 00:02:13.300
At that point, you can ask yourself,

46
00:02:13.300 --> 00:02:17.000
''do I know any techniques that look as though they would work with this problem?''

47
00:02:17.000 --> 00:02:19.510
If they do and you mean look, the problem is solved.

48
00:02:19.510 --> 00:02:21.270
Actually that's usually very unsatisfying

49
00:02:21.270 --> 00:02:23.490
because you've just applied somebody else's thinking.

50
00:02:23.490 --> 00:02:26.490
Mathematics gets interesting when you have to force

51
00:02:26.490 --> 00:02:29.605
yourself to find out when a new method or some new approach.

52
00:02:29.605 --> 00:02:32.355
So, let's assume that none of the techniques you know work.

53
00:02:32.355 --> 00:02:35.840
The next thing is, ''can I adapt an existing technique?''

54
00:02:35.840 --> 00:02:37.790
Maybe you can maybe you can't.

55
00:02:37.790 --> 00:02:41.475
If you can usually leaves you with a nice feeling because you've done something yourself.

56
00:02:41.475 --> 00:02:45.365
The really challenging ones and the ones where it gets actually exciting and fun,

57
00:02:45.365 --> 00:02:47.590
is where it becomes clear,

58
00:02:47.590 --> 00:02:50.520
at least you think that nothing you know is going to help you with that.

59
00:02:50.520 --> 00:02:52.860
At that point, what you do is,

60
00:02:52.860 --> 00:02:58.315
you bash away at it several times with just naive approaches.

61
00:02:58.315 --> 00:02:59.800
Almost certainly they won't work,

62
00:02:59.800 --> 00:03:01.270
you just try this, you try that.

63
00:03:01.270 --> 00:03:04.360
You you come up with ideas based on what the problem is asking you to do,

64
00:03:04.360 --> 00:03:06.470
because solving a problem is a journey.

65
00:03:06.470 --> 00:03:08.855
You begin with the information you've got.

66
00:03:08.855 --> 00:03:10.915
Either the information you're given with the problem,

67
00:03:10.915 --> 00:03:12.810
or information you already know.

68
00:03:12.810 --> 00:03:14.950
You may have to add some extra information,

69
00:03:14.950 --> 00:03:17.520
you may do a Google search find some extra information about it.

70
00:03:17.520 --> 00:03:19.870
At that point, the journey

71
00:03:19.870 --> 00:03:23.365
takes place in that you have to get to the goal and solve the problem.

72
00:03:23.365 --> 00:03:26.695
If none of the methods you try for the first half hour,

73
00:03:26.695 --> 00:03:27.870
maybe I'm depending on the problem.

74
00:03:27.870 --> 00:03:29.415
It could be half hour, couple of hours,

75
00:03:29.415 --> 00:03:31.150
sometimes it's a day or a few weeks.

76
00:03:31.150 --> 00:03:32.815
But if nothing happens,

77
00:03:32.815 --> 00:03:36.395
then the best thing to do is walk away from the problem.

78
00:03:36.395 --> 00:03:37.810
Go and do something else.

79
00:03:37.810 --> 00:03:39.790
In my case, for most of my career,

80
00:03:39.790 --> 00:03:41.365
I was a long-distance runner.

81
00:03:41.365 --> 00:03:43.265
If I verily came up against it,

82
00:03:43.265 --> 00:03:45.350
I would put on my running gear, whatever the whether,

83
00:03:45.350 --> 00:03:46.890
and I'd go out for three or four of hour run,

84
00:03:46.890 --> 00:03:49.655
and when I came back, I felt good,

85
00:03:49.655 --> 00:03:52.510
I felt relaxed, and quite often on the course of that ride,

86
00:03:52.510 --> 00:03:55.225
on that mono, when I got back,

87
00:03:55.225 --> 00:03:56.850
the problem seemed different,

88
00:03:56.850 --> 00:03:58.670
and I had some new ideas.

89
00:03:58.670 --> 00:04:02.730
As I got older, my knees gave away from 25 years of heavy duty running.

90
00:04:02.730 --> 00:04:04.230
These days I've added bicycle.

91
00:04:04.230 --> 00:04:08.825
But I go on my bike, and I go for three or four hour bike ride, and I come back.

92
00:04:08.825 --> 00:04:12.330
First of all, I can approach the problem with a fresh eye.

93
00:04:12.330 --> 00:04:14.475
Secondly, in some cases,

94
00:04:14.475 --> 00:04:17.820
what seemed to be an impossible problem has melted away.

95
00:04:17.820 --> 00:04:19.885
I look at it and think, ''oh, gee,

96
00:04:19.885 --> 00:04:22.240
that was obvious, why didn't I see it before?''

97
00:04:22.240 --> 00:04:23.900
Well, I don't quite think that,

98
00:04:23.900 --> 00:04:27.370
because I know that the feeling you have when you solve a problem is always,

99
00:04:27.370 --> 00:04:31.050
''Ooh, dah, why didn't I think of that before?''

100
00:04:31.050 --> 00:04:34.180
If that doesn't work, you start the cycle again.

101
00:04:34.180 --> 00:04:36.875
You bash away to it again, make no progress,

102
00:04:36.875 --> 00:04:38.850
and then you got to have another bike ride,

103
00:04:38.850 --> 00:04:42.025
or another swim, or whatever it is you'd like to do, a nice long walk.

104
00:04:42.025 --> 00:04:47.940
But it's important to keep oscillating between bashing away at it,

105
00:04:47.940 --> 00:04:53.115
and then backing off and doing something else to let the brain do it's own thing.

106
00:04:53.115 --> 00:04:56.050
I think that was one of the hardest things for me to

107
00:04:56.050 --> 00:04:59.840
realize was how important it is to back away.

108
00:04:59.840 --> 00:05:02.380
Sometimes, especially when it's something difficult,

109
00:05:02.380 --> 00:05:05.710
it is important to be able to focus on it,

110
00:05:05.710 --> 00:05:09.070
as it is to be able to back away from it and then come

111
00:05:09.070 --> 00:05:12.725
back later with a fresh perspective and a fresh mind.

112
00:05:12.725 --> 00:05:15.245
Oh yeah. For difficult problems,

113
00:05:15.245 --> 00:05:18.100
I have never ever solved a difficult problem

114
00:05:18.100 --> 00:05:21.810
sitting at a desk or even sitting in my chair thinking about the problem.

115
00:05:21.810 --> 00:05:24.620
I've never done that, and I'm not sure it's possible to do that,

116
00:05:24.620 --> 00:05:29.535
because if a problem solution requires something new,

117
00:05:29.535 --> 00:05:34.270
applying all of your conscious thinking by definition can't solve it,

118
00:05:34.270 --> 00:05:35.680
because you're looking for something new.

119
00:05:35.680 --> 00:05:38.770
The weird thing about the brain is when you've done

120
00:05:38.770 --> 00:05:42.640
that preparation of bashing away without success,

121
00:05:42.640 --> 00:05:44.605
and you go and do something else.

122
00:05:44.605 --> 00:05:47.700
I've mentioned physical activity because I'm a physical active guy,

123
00:05:47.700 --> 00:05:50.185
although the biggest result in my PhD,

124
00:05:50.185 --> 00:05:52.660
was actually when I was getting out of the bath.

125
00:05:52.660 --> 00:05:54.320
I'd taken a long bath,

126
00:05:54.320 --> 00:05:56.070
you don't have to exert lots of energy.

127
00:05:56.070 --> 00:05:58.270
You have to do something different. Having a bath is

128
00:05:58.270 --> 00:06:01.215
a famous one amongst mathematicians for solving problems.

129
00:06:01.215 --> 00:06:04.650
But somehow, weird stuff happens inside the brain.

130
00:06:04.650 --> 00:06:06.410
When you're doing something else,

131
00:06:06.410 --> 00:06:10.380
and what three hours ago seemed like an impossible problem,

132
00:06:10.380 --> 00:06:13.230
just melts away and everything just falls into place.

133
00:06:13.230 --> 00:06:17.865
The brain sorts it out when it's left alone to do it.

134
00:06:17.865 --> 00:06:23.160
That's exactly the kinds of ideas that we're talking about in in our course.

135
00:06:23.160 --> 00:06:25.870
But it is important to do that initial work.

136
00:06:25.870 --> 00:06:27.630
It's no good just going out for a bike ride,

137
00:06:27.630 --> 00:06:30.060
you've got to have gone through that process of

138
00:06:30.060 --> 00:06:32.940
really coming up against it and seeing everything that doesn't work,

139
00:06:32.940 --> 00:06:36.510
because that seems to set up the mind in

140
00:06:36.510 --> 00:06:40.280
order to do this mysterious stuff it does when you're engaged in something else,

141
00:06:40.280 --> 00:06:42.470
when your conscious mind is engaged in something else.

142
00:06:42.470 --> 00:06:45.050
Absolutely, that's so important.

143
00:06:45.050 --> 00:06:50.260
One thing that you've described is that in higher mathematics,

144
00:06:50.260 --> 00:06:53.760
there should be a lot less doing and a lot more thinking.

145
00:06:53.760 --> 00:06:56.790
Can you tell our viewers what you mean by that,

146
00:06:56.790 --> 00:07:01.235
and how does that relate to areas of learning that are outside mathematics?

147
00:07:01.235 --> 00:07:03.525
Yeah. This is really where you've got to

148
00:07:03.525 --> 00:07:05.990
unlearn lots of the things that you're taught at school.

149
00:07:05.990 --> 00:07:08.700
Actually, teachers do that for very good reasons because

150
00:07:08.700 --> 00:07:11.440
of the way we especially in the United States and parts of Europe,

151
00:07:11.440 --> 00:07:16.110
the way we subject people to endless test-taking with timed tests.

152
00:07:16.110 --> 00:07:22.039
The way to get through the school system is to learn to act fast on the pressure of time,

153
00:07:22.039 --> 00:07:25.270
and that's the last thing you can do when it comes to mathematics.

154
00:07:25.270 --> 00:07:28.755
You've really got to let it take its course.

155
00:07:28.755 --> 00:07:30.220
People have different speeds.

156
00:07:30.220 --> 00:07:33.140
I've worked with some very fast mathematicians.

157
00:07:33.140 --> 00:07:37.235
The young math professor from Stanford who won a Fields Medal recently,

158
00:07:37.235 --> 00:07:39.050
she has gone on record as saying,

159
00:07:39.050 --> 00:07:41.650
her teachers at school thought she wasn't good at mathematics,

160
00:07:41.650 --> 00:07:43.090
because she was so slow.

161
00:07:43.090 --> 00:07:46.010
Well, she may be slow but she now has the equivalent of

162
00:07:46.010 --> 00:07:49.970
a Nobel Prize in mathematics and congratulations to her for doing that.

163
00:07:49.970 --> 00:07:52.440
You really have to let it take its time.

164
00:07:52.440 --> 00:07:53.840
It's a slow process.

165
00:07:53.840 --> 00:07:58.020
You've got to remember that mathematics is relatively recent,

166
00:07:58.020 --> 00:07:59.450
two or three thousand years.

167
00:07:59.450 --> 00:08:04.130
So, you're bringing what's essentially a stone age brain to a domain,

168
00:08:04.130 --> 00:08:05.450
there's a couple of thousand years old.

169
00:08:05.450 --> 00:08:08.260
In fact most mathematics is only a few decades, or so.

170
00:08:08.260 --> 00:08:12.150
How can you take this brain that evolved to survive in

171
00:08:12.150 --> 00:08:16.280
the wild and then more recently to survive in social environments,

172
00:08:16.280 --> 00:08:21.135
how can you take that brain and apply it to solve this abstract problem in mathematics?

173
00:08:21.135 --> 00:08:23.370
Well, the answer is, we don't really know how we do that.

174
00:08:23.370 --> 00:08:26.700
What we do know, is that if you familiarize yourself with

175
00:08:26.700 --> 00:08:31.800
the problem to such an extent that that problem is like a member of your family,

176
00:08:31.800 --> 00:08:34.950
then the act of solving that math problem is actually not

177
00:08:34.950 --> 00:08:37.780
unlike solving a difficult problem you have at home.

178
00:08:37.780 --> 00:08:41.970
We're very good at solving social problems and problems about our environment,

179
00:08:41.970 --> 00:08:44.120
because evolutions set us up for that.

180
00:08:44.120 --> 00:08:47.460
So, somehow we have to figure out how to

181
00:08:47.460 --> 00:08:50.930
let that brain solve that abstract problem in mathematics.

182
00:08:50.930 --> 00:08:55.840
The act of getting inside the problem and thinking about it for a long period of time,

183
00:08:55.840 --> 00:08:57.860
so that it's so familiar.

184
00:08:57.860 --> 00:09:01.180
Then the natural circuits in the brain that

185
00:09:01.180 --> 00:09:05.505
serve us well all of the time actually come into play for a math problem.

186
00:09:05.505 --> 00:09:09.120
There's no proof of that, but that's certainly my own experience of what goes on.

187
00:09:09.120 --> 00:09:15.290
Yes, all I can say is three cheers for slow thinking and I love

188
00:09:15.290 --> 00:09:18.150
the idea of thinking about math and

189
00:09:18.150 --> 00:09:22.440
a difficult problem as being something that's a member of your family.

190
00:09:22.440 --> 00:09:25.230
Yes, in many ways,

191
00:09:25.230 --> 00:09:26.740
mathematic's problems are not

192
00:09:26.740 --> 00:09:30.040
fundamentally different from other problems except in one respect,

193
00:09:30.040 --> 00:09:32.530
they are about totally abstract things.

194
00:09:32.530 --> 00:09:34.740
So someone solving a math problem has

195
00:09:34.740 --> 00:09:38.900
an initial problem that someone doesn't have if they're solving a problem in real life,

196
00:09:38.900 --> 00:09:42.790
or a coaching problem in sort of football.

197
00:09:42.790 --> 00:09:46.295
Most of the time, and I used to spend a lot of time rock-climbing.

198
00:09:46.295 --> 00:09:48.375
Rock-climbing is partly physical,

199
00:09:48.375 --> 00:09:50.145
but it's also a lot of problem solving,

200
00:09:50.145 --> 00:09:51.800
because you're having to look for these moves,

201
00:09:51.800 --> 00:09:53.810
you're having to move your body in the right way.

202
00:09:53.810 --> 00:09:56.170
So there's an awful lot of problem solving that we

203
00:09:56.170 --> 00:09:59.340
do all of the time and that's what the brain evolved to do.

204
00:09:59.340 --> 00:10:01.860
In the case of mathematics however,

205
00:10:01.860 --> 00:10:06.055
the world in which you are solving those problems isn't one we're familiar with.

206
00:10:06.055 --> 00:10:09.625
You initially have to create that world inside of you.

207
00:10:09.625 --> 00:10:14.375
This I think, is why it's essential to knock away at the problem for 10-15 minutes,

208
00:10:14.375 --> 00:10:17.325
a day, two days before you let the brain do it's own thing.

209
00:10:17.325 --> 00:10:21.100
My guess and my perception of what's going on is that

210
00:10:21.100 --> 00:10:25.740
that process is making my mind familiar with that domain,

211
00:10:25.740 --> 00:10:29.750
with that problem, to such an extent that that problem is just like

212
00:10:29.750 --> 00:10:34.310
a problem I'd have with my family or my workplace or whatever.

213
00:10:34.310 --> 00:10:39.010
I've got all of this apparatus for solving real-world problems and social problems.

214
00:10:39.010 --> 00:10:42.390
Once that math problem is inside my mind,

215
00:10:42.390 --> 00:10:44.495
it's another problem just like that.

216
00:10:44.495 --> 00:10:48.450
So, solving the math problem usually isn't the hard part.

217
00:10:48.450 --> 00:10:52.435
The hard part is getting that problem familiar inside your mind.

218
00:10:52.435 --> 00:10:55.250
The only way to do that is just keep living in the problem,

219
00:10:55.250 --> 00:10:56.515
get inside the problem,

220
00:10:56.515 --> 00:10:59.800
really live inside that problem for 10 minutes,

221
00:10:59.800 --> 00:11:02.480
half an hour, maybe even a few days.

222
00:11:02.480 --> 00:11:04.070
In the case of Andrew Wiles,

223
00:11:04.070 --> 00:11:06.515
solving Fermat's last theorem, a few years ago,

224
00:11:06.515 --> 00:11:09.070
it took him seven years to really get inside

225
00:11:09.070 --> 00:11:12.200
that problem to the point where he could see the solution.

226
00:11:12.200 --> 00:11:17.180
Well, part of what you do when you're spending a lot of time with a problem is you're in

227
00:11:17.180 --> 00:11:19.480
some sense reifying and growing

228
00:11:19.480 --> 00:11:23.140
the neural structures that are related to what you're trying to think about.

229
00:11:23.140 --> 00:11:28.400
One of the things that Dr. Sejnowski and I emphasize quite strongly in

230
00:11:28.400 --> 00:11:35.515
our course is how exercise helps actually build neural structures.

231
00:11:35.515 --> 00:11:37.160
It allows you to learn better,

232
00:11:37.160 --> 00:11:39.705
it allows you to remember better.

233
00:11:39.705 --> 00:11:45.750
So how does exercise play a role in your own ability to do mathematics?

234
00:11:45.750 --> 00:11:47.710
That's a good one,

235
00:11:47.710 --> 00:11:48.855
but it certainly is the case for me.

236
00:11:48.855 --> 00:11:50.410
In fact when I was a PhD student,

237
00:11:50.410 --> 00:11:54.800
my closest colleague was a mathematician from the United States,

238
00:11:54.800 --> 00:11:57.200
came over to England when I was a student back in Bristol.

239
00:11:57.200 --> 00:12:00.055
He was a rock climber, in fact I learned to rock climb with him.

240
00:12:00.055 --> 00:12:03.190
We used to go on these three or four hour rock climbs

241
00:12:03.190 --> 00:12:07.235
and we would almost certainly by the time we got to the top of the climb,

242
00:12:07.235 --> 00:12:08.590
we'd done some mathematics.

243
00:12:08.590 --> 00:12:10.990
Because you do a climb and you get to

244
00:12:10.990 --> 00:12:13.800
a ledge and you're tied up and then you chat for a while,

245
00:12:13.800 --> 00:12:15.795
as you're sort of belayed onto the rock face.

246
00:12:15.795 --> 00:12:18.285
We'd talk about mathematics and then we'd forget the mathematics,

247
00:12:18.285 --> 00:12:20.300
we don' do the physical stuff of climbing.

248
00:12:20.300 --> 00:12:23.620
When you're climbing, as when you're bicycling very fast,

249
00:12:23.620 --> 00:12:26.540
your mind is totally focused on that physical activity.

250
00:12:26.540 --> 00:12:29.205
So your entire world is physical,

251
00:12:29.205 --> 00:12:32.820
so you're focused on the climbing or you're focused on riding your bike.

252
00:12:32.820 --> 00:12:35.230
Stuff is going on in the background in your mind,

253
00:12:35.230 --> 00:12:37.510
it's working on those things that you've talked about.

254
00:12:37.510 --> 00:12:40.775
We'd finish a pitch, we'd put ourselves back on belay,

255
00:12:40.775 --> 00:12:44.105
we'd talk about the problem and we've made progress on the problem.

256
00:12:44.105 --> 00:12:45.975
So as a graduate student,

257
00:12:45.975 --> 00:12:49.790
I discovered this method of thinking,

258
00:12:49.790 --> 00:12:52.955
doing, thinking, doing, was incredibly powerful.

259
00:12:52.955 --> 00:12:56.680
I can make conjectures and sort of self

260
00:12:56.680 --> 00:13:00.245
reflect as to why the physical activity is important.

261
00:13:00.245 --> 00:13:06.820
I mean aspects clearly are you're focusing on that activity, it's very refreshing.

262
00:13:06.820 --> 00:13:11.670
We tend to think these days that the mind is separate from the body.

263
00:13:11.670 --> 00:13:14.810
I don't buy that it certainly isn't the case for me.

264
00:13:14.810 --> 00:13:17.310
I find that if my body's tired,

265
00:13:17.310 --> 00:13:20.485
my mind is tired and vice versa they stimulate each other.

266
00:13:20.485 --> 00:13:25.060
So the mind did evolve to help the body survive,

267
00:13:25.060 --> 00:13:28.620
and I think it's a mistake to try and think of those as separate.

268
00:13:28.620 --> 00:13:30.510
If you nurture the body,

269
00:13:30.510 --> 00:13:32.845
the mind follows this goes back

270
00:13:32.845 --> 00:13:36.095
to the ideas of having physical education in schools and so forth.

271
00:13:36.095 --> 00:13:37.290
This is hardly original,

272
00:13:37.290 --> 00:13:39.255
but I think we need to take it seriously.

273
00:13:39.255 --> 00:13:42.605
I've never ever, well I have solved problems in the bath,

274
00:13:42.605 --> 00:13:45.160
that's a somewhat more relaxing case.

275
00:13:45.160 --> 00:13:50.520
But even in the bath, it's an artificial environment in which you're relaxed.

276
00:13:50.520 --> 00:13:54.930
You're in water, you're doing things, you're freeing up the mind,

277
00:13:54.930 --> 00:13:58.050
the mind is absolutely not thinking about that problem,

278
00:13:58.050 --> 00:13:59.605
it's doing other things.

279
00:13:59.605 --> 00:14:03.515
In my case definitely the bath example not withstanding,

280
00:14:03.515 --> 00:14:06.385
physical activity plays a big role.

281
00:14:06.385 --> 00:14:08.910
Exactly why? I don't know,

282
00:14:08.910 --> 00:14:10.425
but I do know it works.

283
00:14:10.425 --> 00:14:13.000
Well there is actually good research evidence.

284
00:14:13.000 --> 00:14:19.205
They're uncovering the bio physiological mechanisms by which it does take an effect.

285
00:14:19.205 --> 00:14:21.510
But I have to ask,

286
00:14:21.510 --> 00:14:23.420
with everything that you do,

287
00:14:23.420 --> 00:14:25.645
do you ever catch yourself,

288
00:14:25.645 --> 00:14:28.470
it's hard to believe, but do you ever procrastinate?

289
00:14:28.470 --> 00:14:31.790
If you do, what do you do to get yourself out of it?

290
00:14:31.790 --> 00:14:35.225
Yeah, well first of all I do do many things,

291
00:14:35.225 --> 00:14:37.640
I've got my own educational technology company,

292
00:14:37.640 --> 00:14:39.205
I do lots of biking.

293
00:14:39.205 --> 00:14:41.365
I run an institute at Stanford,

294
00:14:41.365 --> 00:14:42.954
I do my teaching,

295
00:14:42.954 --> 00:14:44.510
I'm actually on Twitter a lot.

296
00:14:44.510 --> 00:14:45.920
I do a whole bunch of things.

297
00:14:45.920 --> 00:14:48.060
You might think I'm a multi-tasker,

298
00:14:48.060 --> 00:14:50.084
I absolutely do not multitask.

299
00:14:50.084 --> 00:14:53.280
Not least because I know from the research that's been done here at Stanford,

300
00:14:53.280 --> 00:14:55.795
a lot of it by my late colleague Cliff Nass.

301
00:14:55.795 --> 00:14:58.995
That shows that a, human beings cannot multitask,

302
00:14:58.995 --> 00:15:03.020
and b, the more you think you're good at multitasking the worse you are at doing it.

303
00:15:03.020 --> 00:15:07.270
So, I absolutely don't try to use this serial processor to multitask.

304
00:15:07.270 --> 00:15:09.580
What I do is I serial task,

305
00:15:09.580 --> 00:15:14.180
I will work exclusively flat out for periods of time.

306
00:15:14.180 --> 00:15:15.950
Now they may only be 10 minutes,

307
00:15:15.950 --> 00:15:18.505
they may be half an hour or they may be.

308
00:15:18.505 --> 00:15:21.875
I mean recently I had to develop a whole set of 75 puzzles,

309
00:15:21.875 --> 00:15:26.380
a new set of puzzles for my educational video game that we launched last fall.

310
00:15:26.380 --> 00:15:27.965
We wanted to bring out a different version.

311
00:15:27.965 --> 00:15:30.415
I worked nonstop on that for four days.

312
00:15:30.415 --> 00:15:31.960
I didn't do anything else other than eat,

313
00:15:31.960 --> 00:15:34.080
sleep and bicycle ride and in

314
00:15:34.080 --> 00:15:38.350
my entire focus everything else went to one side was to get that thing done.

315
00:15:38.350 --> 00:15:40.890
Four days later I had the 75 puzzles

316
00:15:40.890 --> 00:15:43.925
worked and tested and everything and then I moved to something else.

317
00:15:43.925 --> 00:15:46.660
So what I do is I work very intensely,

318
00:15:46.660 --> 00:15:49.010
and very focused for periods of time.

319
00:15:49.010 --> 00:15:54.000
I mean it's amazing that friends and family are still by me,

320
00:15:54.000 --> 00:15:57.050
because this is very focused and it excludes everything else.

321
00:15:57.050 --> 00:16:00.020
I must have been really difficult to live with at those times.

322
00:16:00.020 --> 00:16:02.805
But everything gets excluded, I focus on it,

323
00:16:02.805 --> 00:16:06.615
and then comes an end either because I finished the job or I just get

324
00:16:06.615 --> 00:16:10.490
tired and want to switch and I'm really getting frustrated with it,

325
00:16:10.490 --> 00:16:12.800
then I'll switch and do something else.

326
00:16:12.800 --> 00:16:16.560
What I've learned to do very efficiently is switch from one to the other,

327
00:16:16.560 --> 00:16:18.430
it doesn't take me more than a few minutes,

328
00:16:18.430 --> 00:16:20.965
at most, to switch from one to the other.

329
00:16:20.965 --> 00:16:26.165
Procrastination comes from recognizing that I need to make that switch.

330
00:16:26.165 --> 00:16:27.955
I'll keep going and keep going I think

331
00:16:27.955 --> 00:16:30.295
I want to do this other thing, I want to do this other thing.

332
00:16:30.295 --> 00:16:34.165
I do find myself doing lots of other little things I'll go and start off

333
00:16:34.165 --> 00:16:38.020
read my Twitter feed and things for a while before making the switch.

334
00:16:38.020 --> 00:16:39.720
So the one moment where I will

335
00:16:39.720 --> 00:16:43.450
procrastinate is starting something new, for obvious reasons.

336
00:16:43.450 --> 00:16:44.680
You've been in the groove,

337
00:16:44.680 --> 00:16:50.010
you've been making progress and it's slowed down and you've got to get in another groove.

338
00:16:50.010 --> 00:16:53.030
I know that that takes willpower and effort and

339
00:16:53.030 --> 00:16:55.910
like everybody else I do have a procrastination streak.

340
00:16:55.910 --> 00:16:58.970
But the only time it affects me is making that switch.

341
00:16:58.970 --> 00:17:02.580
Once I've done it, I'm away in that domain until I've

342
00:17:02.580 --> 00:17:07.410
either solved that issue or done it or completed it or got tired of it and need a break.

343
00:17:07.410 --> 00:17:09.760
That I found works extremely well.

344
00:17:09.760 --> 00:17:13.130
Yeah. That's actually something that we talk about in this course is

345
00:17:13.130 --> 00:17:17.340
just that idea of you don't need to use willpower all the time,

346
00:17:17.340 --> 00:17:21.270
you just need to put it one little place wherever you need,

347
00:17:21.270 --> 00:17:23.060
where you're getting that cue,

348
00:17:23.060 --> 00:17:25.650
that's where you need to be applying willpower.

349
00:17:25.650 --> 00:17:28.205
That seems to be exactly what you do.

350
00:17:28.205 --> 00:17:32.545
It is true that I'm fortunate to have been able to carve out a career for myself,

351
00:17:32.545 --> 00:17:35.650
where most of the time I'm doing stuff I really want to do.

352
00:17:35.650 --> 00:17:39.420
So even though I might not want to write that blog post,

353
00:17:39.420 --> 00:17:42.025
I know at the back of my mind that

354
00:17:42.025 --> 00:17:44.935
the moment I get into it and I've written the first sentences,

355
00:17:44.935 --> 00:17:47.430
writing that blog post will be the most important,

356
00:17:47.430 --> 00:17:51.285
and most wonderful and rewarding thing in the world until it's finished.

357
00:17:51.285 --> 00:17:54.300
But then I have to sort of stop that and do something else,

358
00:17:54.300 --> 00:17:57.990
and that's when there's these hiatus moments when I procrastinate.

359
00:17:57.990 --> 00:18:03.255
It's the switching gears to something else that I find,

360
00:18:03.255 --> 00:18:06.240
I won't say I find it difficult because I've been doing it all my life.

361
00:18:06.240 --> 00:18:08.005
But you do need willpower to do that.

362
00:18:08.005 --> 00:18:10.455
At least I find I need willpower to do that.

363
00:18:10.455 --> 00:18:16.345
If I'm not careful it will just fritter away with social media or whatever.

364
00:18:16.345 --> 00:18:19.970
I feel the same way in how I handle things.

365
00:18:19.970 --> 00:18:26.070
One thing I like to do is I think of my life as like this enormous funnel where there's

366
00:18:26.070 --> 00:18:30.030
all these things that are demanding to be done

367
00:18:30.030 --> 00:18:34.665
but then only one thing can go through that funnel at a time.

368
00:18:34.665 --> 00:18:41.420
So, you just got to give it up and realize one thing is all you can be focusing on.

369
00:18:41.420 --> 00:18:44.130
If you have that approach it makes it a

370
00:18:44.130 --> 00:18:47.510
little easier to realize that you just can't do everything,

371
00:18:47.510 --> 00:18:50.930
you just do as much as you reasonably can.

372
00:18:50.930 --> 00:18:54.080
Yeah. I mean one thing I do do is when I actually

373
00:18:54.080 --> 00:18:57.070
just upgraded my computer system to a to a new Macintosh.

374
00:18:57.070 --> 00:18:58.530
The first thing I did was went into

375
00:18:58.530 --> 00:19:03.180
the system controls and switched off every single alert.

376
00:19:03.180 --> 00:19:04.630
I don't want visual alerts,

377
00:19:04.630 --> 00:19:05.660
I don't want noise alerts.

378
00:19:05.660 --> 00:19:06.910
So I don't want to know if an email has come,

379
00:19:06.910 --> 00:19:08.615
I don't want to know if something is going on.

380
00:19:08.615 --> 00:19:13.940
I literally switch off all the alerts because I do not want things pushed to me,

381
00:19:13.940 --> 00:19:15.855
I want to go and find out things when I need them.

382
00:19:15.855 --> 00:19:19.945
Because the last thing I want if I'm in the middle of working on an article say

383
00:19:19.945 --> 00:19:24.695
is to have some little beep going off to tell me some emails has arrived.

384
00:19:24.695 --> 00:19:25.895
I don't want to know that,

385
00:19:25.895 --> 00:19:29.480
because that's a distraction I want to be in the world of writing that article.

386
00:19:29.480 --> 00:19:33.105
Because if you take the human brain and you put it into a problem,

387
00:19:33.105 --> 00:19:35.770
into a domain and you set it free,

388
00:19:35.770 --> 00:19:37.240
it loves what it's doing,

389
00:19:37.240 --> 00:19:40.490
it doesn't want to give up and that sure doesn't want to be distracted.

390
00:19:40.490 --> 00:19:43.690
So, I don't distract it I just let it do what it does.

391
00:19:43.690 --> 00:19:50.680
Well, I thank you so much Dr. Devlin for sharing your wisdom with us here today.

392
00:19:50.680 --> 00:19:54.565
It's just been a great pleasure talking with you and I

393
00:19:54.565 --> 00:19:58.950
know our viewers will really learn a lot from what you had to say.

394
00:19:58.950 --> 00:20:01.550
Okay the pleasure is been all mine, absolutely.

395
00:20:01.550 --> 00:20:03.700
